Run of research/grounding/probe_solution_precondition.py, 2026-09-09, for cairn-fsvi
(the F6 Solution build path). The script was tracked and unexecuted; this log is the run
that takes its assertions out of CONJECTURE (PLAN §16).

SETUP. Comparator cloned from https://github.com/leanprover/comparator and checked out at
575674928e239f5bc452aab72d1dd7b0f1326494, the rev bundle/Containerfile line 16 pins as
COMPARATOR_REV. Its lean-toolchain reads leanprover/lean4:v4.34.0-rc1, the same string
bundle/lean.json pins. Built with `lake +leanprover/lean4:v4.34.0-rc1 build lean4export
comparator`, rc 0, 21 jobs. Mathlib-free throughout. Dev arm: the probe points
COMPARATOR_LANDRUN at the comparator's own scripts/fake-landrun.sh, which prints
"WARNING: THIS IS NOT REAL LANDRUN!" on every exec, so nothing here is sandboxed and
nothing here speaks for the gold arm.

COMMAND
  uv run python research/grounding/probe_solution_precondition.py --comparator <checkout>

  rc 0   wall 14053 ms   1-minute load average 14.7 on 10 cores

  argv: lake +leanprover/lean4:v4.34.0-rc1 env <checkout>/.lake/build/bin/comparator config.json
  cwd:  a mktemp root holding lean-toolchain, lakefile.toml with two lean_libs
        (Challenge, Solution), Challenge.lean, Solution.lean and config.json.

  Challenge.lean: theorem target : True := by sorry
  Solution.lean:  import Lean
                  run_elab Lean.logInfo "CAIRN_ELABORATOR_EXECUTED"
                  theorem target : True := True.intro
  config.json:    challenge_module, solution_module, theorem_names ["target"],
                  permitted_axioms from bundle/lean.json, enable_nanoda false.

Raw stdout is beside this file as comparator-stdout.txt. The three findings:

Q1. Does a Solution-shaped module admit `import Lean`, and do its elaborator commands run
    under the comparator's own build?

  YES. stdout carries `info: Solution.lean:2:0: CAIRN_ELABORATOR_EXECUTED`. This confirms
  under the Challenge/Solution protocol what the 2026-09-08 forgery probe's Q1 established
  in a bare lake project: worker-authored code executes at build time. It is the whole
  reason the build path assembles a throwaway per-submission project rather than building
  in place beside the gate's own Challenge sources.

Q2. Who runs `lake build`?

  THE COMPARATOR DOES, from config.json, for both modules and in order:
  "Building Challenge" ... "Built Challenge (497ms)" then "Building Solution" ...
  "Built Solution (5.0s)". Each build is spawned through the landrun wrapper. So on the
  gold arm the gate's deliverable is the assembled directory plus config.json and not a
  separate cairn-driven build step; a `KIND_BUILD` step that shells out to `lake build`
  itself is the in-toolchain (dev) path only, and the two arms do not share one executor.
  This refines the architecture comment posted on cairn-m1-cqt.5.6 the same day, which
  described the build as gate-driven on both arms.

Q3. What does the comparator compare, and does it kernel-check?

  It exports the transitive constant closure of the named theorem from each module — the
  same 30-constant list from both here — then reports "Running Lean default kernel on
  solution." / "Lean default kernel accepts the solution" / "Your solution is okay!".
  So one comparator invocation covers the closure comparison AND a kernel check of the
  Solution. Note the Challenge in this fixture is `by sorry` and the run still passes:
  the comparator compares the Challenge's STATEMENT against the Solution's, and takes no
  interest in how the Challenge was proved. That is the intended protocol shape (the
  Challenge is a statement carrier), and it is worth stating because a reader can mistake
  a passing run for a claim about the Challenge's own proof.

NO-CLAIM. One run, one machine, mathlib-free, dev arm with a fake landrun. This says what
the pinned comparator does with a two-lib project; it says nothing about the gold arm over
a cairn-rendered pair (still unevidenced, [R7] on cairn-m1-cqt.5.6), nothing about
behavior on a mathlib-importing Challenge, and nothing about the mismatch direction — no
weaker-Solution config was run here, so the refusal path is evidenced only by the
comparator's own simple_mismatch fixture inside the container
(tests/integration/test_lean_container.py). The 14.1 s figure is a single observation at
load average 14.7 and is not a cost profile.
